---
// Card body for /notes/gauss-newton. Rendered on its own page and inside every series that uses it.
import Tex from '../components/Tex.astro';
---
  <h2 id="problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Many fitting problems have the same shape: a vector of residuals <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{r}(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^m`} />
    (model minus data, at every data point), and we want the parameters <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^n`} /> that make them small:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`f(\mathbf{x}) = \tfrac12\,\|\mathbf{r}(\mathbf{x})\|^2 = \tfrac12\sum_{i=1}^m r_i(\mathbf{x})^2 .`} />
  <p>
    With <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}=\partial\mathbf{r}/\partial\mathbf{x}`} /> the <Tex tex={String.raw`m\times n`} /> Jacobian of the
    residuals, the gradient and the Hessian of <Tex tex={String.raw`f`} /> are
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\nabla f = \mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{r},
    \qquad
    \nabla^2 f = \underbrace{\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{J}}_{\text{first derivatives only}} + \underbrace{\sum_{i} r_i\,\nabla^2 r_i}_{\text{second derivatives, weighted by } r_i} .`} />
  <p>
    Newton's method would solve <Tex tex={String.raw`\nabla^2 f\,\Delta\mathbf{x} = -\nabla f`} /> at every step. That needs the second
    derivatives of every residual, and far from the answer the second term can make the Hessian indefinite, so the
    Newton step may not even point downhill.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gn">Gauss-Newton: drop the second term</h2>
  <p>
    Gauss-Newton simply throws the second term away and solves
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{J}\,\Delta\mathbf{x} = -\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{r} .`} />
  <p>
    There is a cleaner way to see the same step: linearize the residuals, <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{r}(\mathbf{x}+\Delta\mathbf{x})\approx\mathbf{r}+\mathbf{J}\Delta\mathbf{x}`} />,
    and solve the resulting <em>linear</em> least-squares problem <Tex tex={String.raw`\min_{\Delta\mathbf{x}}\|\mathbf{r}+\mathbf{J}\Delta\mathbf{x}\|^2`} />
    exactly. Its normal equations are the line above. Three things come for free:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Only first derivatives.</strong> You need <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}`} />, which you need for the gradient anyway.</li>
    <li><strong>Always downhill.</strong> <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{J}`} /> is positive semidefinite, so when
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}`} /> has full column rank the step is a descent direction, wherever you are.</li>
    <li><strong>A well-posed solve.</strong> In practice you can solve the least-squares problem with a QR factorization of
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}`} /> instead of forming <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{J}`} />, which squares the condition number.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="when">When is dropping it harmless?</h2>
  <p>
    The dropped term is weighted by the residuals <Tex tex={String.raw`r_i`} />. So the approximation is good exactly when the
    residuals are small at the answer, or when the model is nearly linear (small <Tex tex={String.raw`\nabla^2 r_i`} />).
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zero-residual problems</strong> (the model can fit the data exactly): near the solution Gauss-Newton converges
      quadratically, just like Newton. On the demo problem below with the misfit slider at zero, starting 0.07 away
      from the fit, the error drops 7&nbsp;×&nbsp;10<sup>−2</sup> → 5&nbsp;×&nbsp;10<sup>−4</sup> → 7&nbsp;×&nbsp;10<sup>−8</sup>
      → 3&nbsp;×&nbsp;10<sup>−15</sup>.</li>
    <li><strong>Large-residual problems</strong> (the model cannot follow the data): convergence becomes linear, with a
      rate that gets worse as the residuals grow. From the same nearby start, at misfit 1 the error shrinks by about
      a factor of 12 per step, at misfit 3 only by about 4, while Newton stays quadratic. For very large residuals, plain
      Gauss-Newton can fail to converge at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="variants">The variants</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Line search (damped Gauss-Newton).</strong> Keep the direction, but shorten the step until the loss actually
    decreases enough (a backtracking or Armijo line search). Because the direction is always downhill, this always
    finds a step.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Levenberg-Marquardt.</strong> Add a multiple of the identity before solving:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\left(\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{J} + \mu\,\mathbf{I}\right)\Delta\mathbf{x} = -\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{r} .`} />
  <p>
    With <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu\to 0`} /> this is Gauss-Newton; with large <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu`} /> it is a short gradient-descent step,
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\Delta\mathbf{x}\approx-\nabla f/\mu`} />. The damping is adapted on the fly: shrink
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu`} /> when a step reduces the loss about as much as the linear model predicted, grow it when the step
    fails. It is a trust-region method in disguise, and it also fixes a rank-deficient <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}`} />.
    Marquardt's original version scales the damping by the diagonal of <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{J}`} /> instead of the
    identity, which makes it insensitive to the units of each parameter.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Generalized Gauss-Newton.</strong> The same trick works whenever the loss is a nice convex function
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\phi`} /> of some intermediate quantity, <Tex tex={String.raw`f(\mathbf{x}) = \phi(\mathbf{r}(\mathbf{x}))`} />: keep
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\nabla^2\phi\,\mathbf{J}`} /> and drop the term with second derivatives of
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{r}`} />. In a simulation-based inverse problem, <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{r}`} /> is the simulated
    state and <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}`} /> is its sensitivity to the parameters. You rarely form
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}`} /> there: a conjugate-gradient solve only needs products <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}\mathbf{v}`} />
    (one forward-sensitivity pass) and <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{w}`} /> (one <a href="/notes/adjoint-method">adjoint</a> pass).
  </p>

  <h2 id="poking">In practice: fitting a material by poking</h2>
  <p>
    This is the optimizer we used to measure nonlinear materials in
    <a href="#references">Chen, Zhao and Barbič (2023)</a>. We poke a layer of material with rigid cylinders of several
    radii and record the contact force at each indentation depth (Section 3). The material is a stretch-based
    Valanis–Landel energy whose shape is set by a parameter vector <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\mu}`} />: the values of
    a cubic spline for <Tex tex={String.raw`f''`} /> at a set of control points, plus a Lamé parameter for the volume term
    (Sections 6 and 7). For every poking sample <Tex tex={String.raw`i`} />, a quasi-static FEM solve gives the simulated total
    contact force <Tex tex={String.raw`f^S_i`} />, and the loss is a sum of squares against the measured force
    <Tex tex={String.raw`f^E_i`} /> (Equation 13), plus a small smoothness penalty on the spline (Equation 17):
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\ell(\boldsymbol{\mu}) = \sum_i \big(f^S_i(\boldsymbol{\mu}) - f^E_i\big)^2 + \frac{\beta}{2}\,\boldsymbol{\mu}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{L}\,\boldsymbol{\mu}.`} />
  <p>
    The residuals here are one number per poking sample, and the parameters are the spline values. The paper writes
    out the full Hessian of the force term (Equation 15, derived in Appendix E). Its first line is the
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf{J}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{J}`} /> term above, and its second line is the residual-weighted term that
    Gauss-Newton drops. Appendix E records what we found: useful optimizations can be obtained with as little as the
    first term, but not without it. That first-term-only optimizer is the one labeled Gauss-Newton in the paper's
    Figure 10, where it converges faster than BFGS and L-BFGS on our problem. Before that, we had tried nonlinear
    conjugate gradients, which converged more slowly or not at all (Section 7). The smoothness term is already a
    quadratic in <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\mu}`} />, so its Hessian <Tex tex={String.raw`\beta\mathbf{L}`} /> is exact; and the stability
    condition <Tex tex={String.raw`f''>0`} /> is enforced by clamping <Tex tex={String.raw`f''`} /> to a small positive value.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The "Fit E" trick.</strong> Our material energy is linear in <Tex tex={String.raw`\boldsymbol{\mu}`} />. So if you scale
    every parameter by the same <Tex tex={String.raw`\kappa>0`} />, the elastic forces scale by <Tex tex={String.raw`\kappa`} /> at every shape:
    the equilibrium shape under the cylinder does not change, and every simulated contact force is simply multiplied
    by <Tex tex={String.raw`\kappa`} />. That makes <Tex tex={String.raw`\ell(\kappa\boldsymbol{\mu})`} /> a scalar quadratic in
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\kappa`} />, which we minimize exactly after every iteration at almost no cost:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\kappa^\ast = \frac{\sum_i f^S_i f^E_i}{\sum_i (f^S_i)^2 + \tfrac{\beta}{2}\,\boldsymbol{\mu}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf{L}\boldsymbol{\mu}},\qquad \boldsymbol{\mu}\leftarrow\kappa^\ast\boldsymbol{\mu}.`} />
  <p>
    Physically, this re-fits the overall stiffness (the Young's modulus, hence the name) while keeping the shape of
    the material curve. In the paper it cut the number of Newton iterations by about a factor of two
    (Section 7, Figure 10). The same idea gives the starting point: restrict the material to a constant
    <Tex tex={String.raw`f''`} />, so that only Young's modulus and Poisson's ratio remain, find the best Young's modulus for each
    Poisson's ratio with the same scaling step, and minimize over Poisson's ratio alone. Across the four real materials
    in the paper, the whole fit took 10 to 54 iterations (Table 1).
  </p>
  <p>
    The general lesson: when some parameters enter the model linearly, solve for them exactly in an inner step and
    let the outer optimizer handle the rest. (This is related to what is known as variable projection.) The demo below
    has the same structure, since the curve <Tex tex={String.raw`a\,e^{bt}`} /> is linear in <Tex tex={String.raw`a`} />.
  </p>

  <h2 id="demo">Try it</h2>
  <p>
    The demo fits the curve <Tex tex={String.raw`y = a\,e^{b t}`} /> to five data points, so there are two parameters and the
    loss can be drawn as a contour map over <Tex tex={String.raw`(a, b)`} />. <strong>Click or drag on the map</strong> to choose a
    starting point; all methods start there. The <strong>misfit</strong> slider bends the data away from any
    exponential, which raises the residuals at the best fit. The lower plot shows the data and each method's final curve.
  </p>
  <figure class="note-fig gn-demo">
    <canvas id="gn-map" width="880" height="600" aria-label="Contour map of the least-squares loss over the two parameters, with the paths of four optimization methods."></canvas>
    <canvas id="gn-fit" width="880" height="220" aria-label="The data points and the fitted curves."></canvas>
    <div class="gn-controls">
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-m="gd" checked /> <span class="sw" data-c="gd"></span> Gradient descent</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-m="gn" checked /> <span class="sw" data-c="gn"></span> Gauss-Newton</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-m="lm" checked /> <span class="sw" data-c="lm"></span> Levenberg-Marquardt</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" data-m="newton" checked /> <span class="sw" data-c="newton"></span> Newton</label>
    </div>
    <div class="gn-controls">
      <label>Misfit <input type="range" id="gn-mis" min="0" max="3" step="0.25" value="0" /> <span id="gn-misv">0</span></label>
      <label>LM starting damping μ <input type="range" id="gn-mu" min="-3" max="3" step="0.5" value="0" /> <span id="gn-muv">1</span></label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="gn-ls" /> Line search for Gauss-Newton and Newton</label>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="gn-fit-a" /> Refit <Tex tex={String.raw`a`} /> exactly after each step ("Fit E")</label>
    </div>
    <div class="gn-read" id="gn-read"></div>
    <figcaption>Shaded bands are level sets of the loss (on a log scale); the cross marks the best fit. Each path is one
      method's iterates. A method "converges" when the gradient falls below 10<sup>−9</sup>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Things to try. At misfit 0, Gauss-Newton and Levenberg-Marquardt reach the fit in 5 to 8 steps from almost
    anywhere, while gradient descent needs close to 200, zigzagging down the narrow valley. Pure Newton often
    walks off the map: far from the fit, the full Hessian has a negative eigenvalue (it does at four of the five
    starting points I tested), and the step goes the wrong way. Raise the misfit to 3: Gauss-Newton still
    converges, but needs more steps. Push the starting damping of Levenberg-Marquardt up and its first steps
    look like gradient descent; it then speeds up as the damping is reduced.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then turn on the line search and the exact refit of <Tex tex={String.raw`a`} />, the analogue of "Fit E". Here it cuts
    gradient descent's steps by a factor of two to three (from 188 to 83 at the default start): after every step it
    lands exactly on the floor of the valley in the <Tex tex={String.raw`a`} /> direction.
    Gauss-Newton barely changes in this two-parameter toy; it already handles the scale well. Without the line search,
    the refit cannot rescue a Gauss-Newton step that overshoots, so keep both on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checklist">A short checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Start with Levenberg-Marquardt</strong> (or Gauss-Newton with a line search) for any sum-of-squares loss.
      Pure Newton is rarely worth its second derivatives far from the answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Look at the residual at the solution.</strong> If it stays large and convergence is slow, the dropped term
      matters: consider adding it back near the end, or a quasi-Newton correction.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid forming J transpose J</strong> when the problem is ill-conditioned; use QR, or conjugate gradients
      with Jacobian-vector products.</li>
    <li><strong>Scale your parameters.</strong> Gradient descent and plain damping are sensitive to units; Marquardt's
      diagonal scaling helps.</li>
    <li><strong>Solve linear parameters exactly.</strong> If the model is linear in some parameters (an overall
      stiffness, a scale), minimize over them in closed form after every step, as with "Fit E".</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="references">References</h2>
  <ul class="refs">
    <li>Huanyu Chen, Danyong Zhao, Jernej Barbič. Capturing Animation-Ready Isotropic Materials Using Systematic Poking.
      <em>ACM Transactions on Graphics</em> 42(6) (SIGGRAPH Asia 2023).
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3618406">doi:10.1145/3618406</a></li>
  </ul>


<style>
  .gn-demo canvas { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; }
  #gn-map { touch-action: none; cursor: crosshair; }
  #gn-fit { margin-top: .5em; }
  .gn-controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .4em 1.2em; align-items: center; margin-top: .6em; font-size: .95em; }
  .gn-controls input[type=range] { vertical-align: middle; }
  .sw { display: inline-block; width: 1.4em; height: .3em; vertical-align: middle; border-radius: 2px; }
  .gn-read { margin-top: .6em; font-size: .9em; }
  .gn-read table { border-collapse: collapse; }
  .gn-read td { padding: .1em 1em .1em 0; }
</style>

<script>
  import { data, f, run } from '../scripts/gauss-newton.js';
  const map = document.getElementById('gn-map') as HTMLCanvasElement, mc = map.getContext('2d')!;
  const fit = document.getElementById('gn-fit') as HTMLCanvasElement, fc = fit.getContext('2d')!;
  const W = map.width, H = map.height;
  const A0 = 0, A1 = 3.5, B0 = -1.5, B1 = 3; // parameter window: a horizontal, b vertical
  const px = (a: number) => ((a - A0) / (A1 - A0)) * W, py = (b: number) => H - ((b - B0) / (B1 - B0)) * H;
  const pa = (x: number) => A0 + (x / W) * (A1 - A0), pb = (y: number) => B0 + ((H - y) / H) * (B1 - B0);
  const NAMES: Record<string, string> = { gd: 'Gradient descent', gn: 'Gauss-Newton', lm: 'Levenberg-Marquardt', newton: 'Newton' };
  let start = [0.5, 0.2], misfit = 0, mu0 = 1, ls = false, rescale = false;
  const on: Record<string, boolean> = { gd: true, gn: true, lm: true, newton: true };
  const css = (v: string) => getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(v).trim() || '#000';
  const dark = () => matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
  const color = (m: string) => ({ gd: css('--muted'), gn: css('--link'), lm: dark() ? '#5cc6a8' : '#1f7a62', newton: dark() ? '#f0a848' : '#b25f00' } as Record<string, string>)[m];
  let bg: ImageData | null = null, bgKey = '';

  function shade() {
    const key = misfit + (dark() ? 'd' : 'l');
    if (bg && bgKey === key) { mc.putImageData(bg, 0, 0); return; }
    const Y = data(misfit), ink = dark() ? [232, 232, 232] : [26, 26, 26], back = dark() ? [20, 20, 20] : [255, 255, 255];
    const img = mc.createImageData(W, H), C = 4;
    for (let y = 0; y < H; y += C) for (let x = 0; x < W; x += C) {
      const v = Math.log(f([pa(x + C / 2), pb(y + C / 2)], Y) + 1e-3) * 1.6, band = Math.floor(v) & 1 ? 0.10 : 0.03;
      for (let j = 0; j < C; j++) for (let i = 0; i < C; i++) {
        const k = ((y + j) * W + (x + i)) * 4;
        for (let c = 0; c < 3; c++) img.data[k + c] = back[c] * (1 - band) + ink[c] * band;
        img.data[k + 3] = 255;
      }
    }
    mc.putImageData(img, 0, 0); bg = mc.getImageData(0, 0, W, H); bgKey = key;
  }

  function drawPath(p: number[][], col: string) {
    mc.strokeStyle = col; mc.fillStyle = col; mc.lineWidth = 2.2; mc.beginPath();
    p.forEach((x, i) => { const X = px(x[0]), Yp = py(x[1]); i ? mc.lineTo(X, Yp) : mc.moveTo(X, Yp); }); mc.stroke();
    for (const x of p) { mc.beginPath(); mc.arc(px(x[0]), py(x[1]), 2.6, 0, 7); mc.fill(); }
  }

  function draw() {
    const Y = data(misfit), ink = css('--ink'), muted = css('--muted');
    shade();
    const best = run('lm', [1.5, 1], Y, { mu0: 1 }, 2000, 1e-13).at(-1)!;
    const res: Record<string, number[][]> = {};
    for (const m of ['gd', 'gn', 'lm', 'newton']) res[m] = run(m, start, Y, { lineSearch: ls, mu0, rescale }, 500, 1e-9);
    for (const m of ['gd', 'newton', 'lm', 'gn']) if (on[m]) drawPath(res[m], color(m));
    mc.strokeStyle = ink; mc.lineWidth = 2; const bx = px(best[0]), by = py(best[1]);
    mc.beginPath(); mc.moveTo(bx - 8, by - 8); mc.lineTo(bx + 8, by + 8); mc.moveTo(bx - 8, by + 8); mc.lineTo(bx + 8, by - 8); mc.stroke();
    mc.fillStyle = ink; mc.beginPath(); mc.arc(px(start[0]), py(start[1]), 6, 0, 7); mc.fill();
    mc.font = '14px system-ui, sans-serif'; mc.fillStyle = muted;
    mc.fillText('a →', W - 40, H - 10); mc.fillText('b ↑', 8, 18);
    // fit panel
    const FW = fit.width, FH = fit.height; fc.fillStyle = css('--bg'); fc.fillRect(0, 0, FW, FH);
    const T0 = -0.05, T1 = 1.05, Ymin = Math.min(0, ...Y) - 0.5, Ymax = Math.max(...Y) + 1;
    const fx = (t: number) => ((t - T0) / (T1 - T0)) * FW, fy = (v: number) => FH - ((v - Ymin) / (Ymax - Ymin)) * FH;
    for (const m of ['gd', 'newton', 'lm', 'gn']) if (on[m]) {
      const x = res[m].at(-1)!; if (!isFinite(x[0]) || !isFinite(x[1])) continue;
      fc.strokeStyle = color(m); fc.lineWidth = 2; fc.beginPath();
      for (let k = 0; k <= 100; k++) { const t = T0 + (k / 100) * (T1 - T0), v = x[0] * Math.exp(x[1] * t); k ? fc.lineTo(fx(t), fy(v)) : fc.moveTo(fx(t), fy(v)); }
      fc.stroke();
    }
    fc.fillStyle = ink; [0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1].forEach((t, i) => { fc.beginPath(); fc.arc(fx(t), fy(Y[i]), 5, 0, 7); fc.fill(); });
    // readout
    const rows = ['gd', 'gn', 'lm', 'newton'].filter((m) => on[m]).map((m) => {
      const p = res[m], e = p.at(-1)!, ok = Math.hypot(e[0] - best[0], e[1] - best[1]) < 1e-5;
      const label = ok ? `${p.length - 1} steps` : (p.length > 500 ? 'not converged after 500 steps' : 'did not reach the fit');
      return `<tr><td><span class="sw" style="background:${color(m)}"></span> ${NAMES[m]}</td><td>${label}</td></tr>`;
    }).join('');
    (document.getElementById('gn-read') as HTMLElement).innerHTML = `Best fit a = ${best[0].toFixed(3)}, b = ${best[1].toFixed(3)}, loss ${f(best, Y).toFixed(3)}<table>${rows}</table>`;
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.gn-controls .sw').forEach((s) => (s.style.background = color(s.dataset.c!)));
  }

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLInputElement>('.gn-controls input[data-m]').forEach((c) => c.addEventListener('change', () => { on[c.dataset.m!] = c.checked; draw(); }));
  const mis = document.getElementById('gn-mis') as HTMLInputElement, mu = document.getElementById('gn-mu') as HTMLInputElement;
  mis.addEventListener('input', () => { misfit = +mis.value; (document.getElementById('gn-misv') as HTMLElement).textContent = mis.value; draw(); });
  mu.addEventListener('input', () => { mu0 = 10 ** +mu.value; (document.getElementById('gn-muv') as HTMLElement).textContent = mu0 >= 1 ? String(Math.round(mu0)) : mu0.toPrecision(1); draw(); });
  (document.getElementById('gn-ls') as HTMLInputElement).addEventListener('change', (e) => { ls = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).checked; draw(); });
  (document.getElementById('gn-fit-a') as HTMLInputElement).addEventListener('change', (e) => { rescale = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).checked; draw(); });
  let dragging = false;
  const setStart = (e: PointerEvent) => { const r = map.getBoundingClientRect(); start = [pa(((e.clientX - r.left) / r.width) * W), pb(((e.clientY - r.top) / r.height) * H)]; draw(); };
  map.addEventListener('pointerdown', (e) => { dragging = true; map.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId); setStart(e); });
  map.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => { if (dragging) setStart(e); });
  map.addEventListener('pointerup', () => (dragging = false));
  matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change', () => { bg = null; draw(); });
  draw();
</script>
